story += [P('Temporal TV Recommendation on SWEET.TV', 'TitleRu'), P('Petr Vorozhtsov | Individual | 2026-10-24'), P('Email: pavorozhtsov@edu.hse.ru | Assignment: LLM4Rec Midterm Quiz (GSB)'), P('Kaggle: PetrVorozhtsovLMS')]
p('<link href="https://github.com/PetrVorozhtsov/RecSys-LLMs/tree/exam-sweettv/exam_sweettv" color="blue">Repository: RecSys-LLMs / exam-sweettv / exam_sweettv</link>')
h('Abstract')
p('SWEET.TV requires five ranked programme recommendations per test user from viewing logs whose schedule labels are sparse. This study joins viewing intervals to labelled broadcasts and compares content-based filtering and association scoring with a popularity reference on a temporal hold-out. Popularity achieves local MAP@5 of 0.162263, compared with 0.157865 for association and 0.008688 for content, while the submitted association file returns a Kaggle private score of 0.01454. Popularity is the strongest measured launch candidate, and the submitted personalised model requires further work on cold-start evaluation, metadata timing and association normalisation.')
p('Index Terms: recommender systems; implicit feedback; temporal validation; association rules; content-based filtering.')
h('1. Introduction')
p('The prediction target is a ranked Top-5 list of programme IDs for each of 1,260 users in weeks 31-42. Earlier viewing intervals identify implicit interactions, not explicit satisfaction. Only about 10.8% of schedule rows have a programme ID [1], so matching quality and coverage constrain every recommender before ranking begins.')
p('For example, an interval on channel c is matched to a labelled broadcast on the same channel; its overlap seconds enter the user profile. For user 8377619604347126107, the submitted association list starts with 2500413 (Civil Defence). This illustrates how an interval-level log becomes a ranked programme-level result. Contributions are a reproducible join-and-ranking pipeline, a shared temporal comparison of two personalised methods plus baseline, and an evidence-based analysis of two fixed users.')
h('2. Related work and alternatives')
p('Cosine similarity compares normalised feature vectors [2], matching the content implementation. Association confidence and lift describe conditional co-occurrence [3]; the current lift-like weighting differs from the classical definition, as documented below. Personalised PageRank propagates preference through a graph [4], while implicit ALS learns low-rank factors from confidence-weighted interactions [5]. These references were consulted during the report audit; scikit-learn, mlxtend, NetworkX and implicit were not dependencies of the submitted pipeline.')
p('Alternatives considered: implicit ALS could share statistical strength across sparse histories but needs tuning of factors and confidence weights; personalised PageRank could exploit multi-hop co-view paths but adds graph construction and convergence choices. Neither was implemented or benchmarked, so no superiority over the measured methods is claimed. Content and direct pair scoring were chosen for their transparent features and bounded implementation cost.')
h('Study scope')
p('The submitted file remains the association solution. The final selector compared association with content, excluding popularity from selection. This explains the submitted choice but does not justify claiming it is best overall. The launch recommendation in Section 5 follows the baseline result instead.')
story.append(PageBreak())
h('3. Method')
p('Pipeline: read labelled schedules -> retain template users -> join intervals -> aggregate user-item overlap -> fit on weeks 11-20 -> evaluate weeks 21-30 -> combine user histories for final ranking -> write five IDs per user. No test-period viewing behaviour is supplied or used. Validation interactions are not used to fit the local popularity, profile or pair models.')
h('3.1. Join and data coverage')
p('dataset11-30.csv contains 5,821,987 views. vsetv_id is matched to channel_id. Viewing bounds come from start_time and stop_time; duraton is viewing length, not programme duration. Blank and zero programme IDs are dropped and cannot become items. For equal channels, overlap = min(stop_view, stop_show) - max(start_view, start_show). A view counts when overlap is at least 60 seconds and overlap / schedule_duration is at least 0.10. The eligible broadcast with maximum overlap is selected; repeated matches accumulate seconds per user-item pair.')
p('The absolute threshold rejects brief switches; the fractional threshold accounts for programme length. Compared with the 396-second median view reported in the assignment, 60 seconds is a modest absolute cutoff. Neither threshold was tuned. A long session spanning several broadcasts loses all but one match; long programmes may fail the 10% condition. This is a known trade-off, not an empirically optimal join.')
table([['Processing audit','Count'],['Input views / excluded non-template views','5,821,987 / 3,648,674'],['Unmatched or unlabelled / matched','1,671,100 / 502,213'],['Train-history users / combined-history users','1,250 / 1,258'],['Final catalogue / submission users','9,671 / 1,260']], [110*mm,65*mm])
p('Matched intervals are 23.11% of the 2,173,313 retained views, not 23.11% of all schedule rows. The audit counts intervals, not distinct user-item pairs. Restricting all model fitting to template users reduces the collaborative signal available from the wider audience.')
h('3.2. Ranking and configuration')
table([['Method','Actual scoring rule and parameters'],['Popularity','Rank by distinct training viewers per ID. Ties follow input-dependent Counter ordering.'],['Content','Category weight 2; genre 1; production decade 0.5; actor/director tokens longer than two characters 0.25. User vector is the sum of item vectors weighted by log(1 + overlap_seconds); rank by cosine similarity, then string ID.'],['Association','Build co-view pairs from the first 60 distinct IDs in insertion order per user. confidence(a,b) = pair_count(a,b) / item_users(a). Contribution: log(1 + overlap_user_a) * confidence(a,b) * log(1 + lift_like).'],['Lift-like term','confidence(a,b) / (item_users(b) / sum(item_users.values())). The denominator is total item support, not the number of users; this is not classical lift [3].'],['Output','Scan the method ranking, then popularity; remove previously watched IDs and within-list duplicates; stop at five IDs. Scores are rankings, not interchangeable probabilities.']], [31*mm,144*mm])
h('3.3. Candidates, repeats and cold start')
p('Validation candidates combine labelled IDs from weeks 11-20 and 21-30. Final candidates combine weeks 11-42, rather than restricting to test-period airings. Watched IDs are blocked identically across methods, although validation truth retains repeat views. For empty histories, the submitted association ranking is empty and returns the Top-5 popularity list; popularity does the same. The current content code instead returns five zero-score candidates in string-ID order. Thus the required identical fallback across all methods is not fully satisfied; this limitation is disclosed rather than hidden.')
story.append(PageBreak())
h('4. Experiments and verification')
p('Protocol: train on weeks 11-20 and validate on weeks 21-30; K = 5 throughout. This forward split reflects the later test window. AP@5 sums Precision@r at relevant ranks and divides by min(5, number of relevant IDs); MAP@5 averages across users with nonempty validation truth. Users absent from train remain in the denominator but receive empty predictions, a cold-start evaluation defect shared across the reported methods.')
table([['Method','Temporal validation MAP@5','Difference from popularity'],['Popularity','0.162263','Reference'],['Association','0.157865','-0.004398'],['Content','0.008688','-0.153575']], [48*mm,64*mm,63*mm])
p('Association is approximately 2.71% below popularity relative to the baseline score. Content is much weaker. This supports popularity as the next launch baseline; it does not establish the causes of the gap. No confidence intervals, hyperparameter sweep or ablation were performed. The association submission has Kaggle Private Score 0.01454 and Public Score 0.01740; local and Kaggle values use different windows and targets and are not directly comparable.')
h('4.1. Environment and measured evidence')
p(f'Windows; Python {platform.python_version()}; CPU identifier: {escape(verified["cpu"])}; {verified["logical_cpus"]} logical CPUs; {verified["ram_gib"]} GiB installed physical memory. run.py uses only the Python standard library. PDF generation uses ReportLab {reportlab.Version}; rendering checks use Poppler. No random seed or PYTHONHASHSEED was set. No stochastic optimiser is used, but set iteration can affect insertion order and tied association scores; exact cross-process reproducibility is not assumed.')
if reproduced:
    p(f'Independent audit rerun of the unchanged pipeline: {reproduced["pipeline_seconds"]:.2f} seconds wall-clock, exit code {reproduced["returncode"]}. Output comparisons: '+escape('; '.join(f'{name}: {"byte-identical" if same else "different"}' for name,same in reproduced['identical'].items()))+'. This is a measured rerun, not the unrecorded original runtime. See outputs/reproduction_verification.json and reproduction.log.')
else:
    p('The original full-run time was not recorded. A timed reproduction was started during the audit; its result is not claimed until it completes. Peak memory was not measured.')
p('Verification script verify_report.py passed: exact template header and user order, 1,260 rows, exactly five distinct IDs per row, and six example lists of five IDs with no overlap with their saved user histories. The checks also independently compared the code metric with this worked example: relevant {A,C}, predictions [A,B,C,D,E], AP@5 = (1 + 2/3) / 2 = 0.8333333333. This is an assistant-run check; the student has not confirmed personally hand-computing it.')
h('5. Discussion: strategy, failures and improvement')
p('<b>My platform choice:</b> I would begin with popularity because it has the highest observed local MAP@5, low computational cost and a simple reserve for users without history. I would test association next as a transparent personalisation candidate; its current submitted score is not evidence that it beats popularity. This first-person explanation was drafted with AI assistance and remains subject to my own review.')
p('<b>What worked:</b> the pipeline produced a scoreable Top-5 file, both required personalised methods were evaluated, and user-specific lists can be traced to exact IDs. <b>What did not work:</b> content failed to match the baseline; association preserved all five baseline IDs for the second example user; and a cold-start probe exposed inconsistent content behaviour. Popularity is cheap to aggregate; content scans candidates per user; pair construction is capped at 1,770 unordered pairs per user before aggregation.')
p('<b>Observed failure and diagnostic:</b> an empty content profile returns [1,2,3,4,5] on the audit toy catalogue rather than the prescribed popularity list [10,20,30,40,50]. The cause is zero cosine scores sorted by ID. A proposed fix is to route empty or zero-norm profiles directly to popularity and generate validation predictions for every validation user. This model fix has not been applied or benchmarked; the report fix is to remove the earlier false claim of a shared fallback. The failing behaviour was confirmed in outputs/report_verification.json.')
p('<b>Next change:</b> repair the common cold-start branch and rerun the same hold-out before changing another variable. Separately, freeze metadata at the training cutoff: the current content features merge future-schedule attributes, which exceeds strict availability-only access. The association pair table also remains trained on weeks 11-20 while final profiles use weeks 11-30. These are unresolved implementation limitations; no full-compliance claim is made.')
story.append(PageBreak())
h('5.1. Two users: recommendations against observed histories')
p('The first two template users are a reproducible, non-cherry-picked choice; both have usable histories with different category mixes. They are not representative of all users or cold start. Histories cover weeks 11-30; future truth is unavailable. English titles are display translations from schedule metadata; IDs retain source identity.')
for user,data in examples.items():
    h(f'User {user}: {len(data["history"])} historical programmes')
    cats=Counter(cat_en(sid) for sid in data['history'])
    p('Historical category counts: '+escape('; '.join(f'{k}: {v}' for k,v in cats.most_common()))+'. Counts measure unique IDs, not viewing-time shares.')
    p('Observed history examples: '+escape('; '.join(f'{sid}: {title_en(sid)} ({cat_en(sid)})' for sid in data['history'][:4]))+'.')
    rows=[['Method','Ordered Top-5: ID, English title (category)']]
    for method,preds in data['predictions'].items():
        rows.append([method,'; '.join(f'{n}. {sid}: {title_en(sid)} ({cat_en(sid)})' for n,sid in enumerate(preds[:5],1))])
    table(rows,[25*mm,150*mm])
    if user==list(examples)[0]:
        p('Content returns five films, aligned with the largest historical category (35 of 90 IDs), but category alignment alone cannot establish relevance. Popularity returns three entertainment shows, one information show and one series; association replaces two of those IDs and shares three of five. These lists reflect additional historical categories (19 entertainment, 7 information, 6 series), while offering unseen IDs within the logged history.')
    else:
        p('Content returns four films and one entertainment show; films are the largest historical category (13 of 53 IDs). Popularity and association share all five IDs, with the last two positions exchanged: both contain three entertainment, one information and one educational programme. Those categories occur in the history (11, 7 and 5 IDs). For this user the association model changes ordering rather than expanding the recommended set.')
p('All six lists contain five IDs, with no ID in the corresponding saved history. This follows from the explicit filter, not from proof of useful discovery. Familiar categories can support plausibility, but an unfamiliar ID may still be a poor recommendation; the log does not show enjoyment or viewing on other platforms. Future per-user hits cannot be checked here.')
h('5.2. Why the approaches differ')
p('Association represents explicit pairwise co-view evidence and conditional direction through confidence. Low-rank factorisation [5] shares information through latent factors and need not preserve an individual observed rule; association is not inherently more expressive and cannot replace latent generalisation. Content can match category, genre, people and decade even when a programme has no co-view links, but can overconcentrate on broad attributes. Personalised PageRank [4] would spread preference across multiple graph steps and redistribute mass through restart, potentially reaching programmes without a direct pair link to a watched ID. Neither ALS nor PageRank was evaluated here, so these are mechanism comparisons, not measured rankings. Popularity already works well both in aggregate MAP@5 and in the second user’s shared Top-5 set.')
story.append(PageBreak())
